# rules: kotlin-trusts-any-certificate
# languages: kotlin
# fixture: kotlin/CWE-295.kt
# from: CWE-295 (Improper Certificate Validation)
#
# There is no legitimate empty `checkServerTrusted`. The method is called with
# the chain the server presented and is expected to throw when it does not like
# it; a body that does nothing says every chain is acceptable, which is the
# same as no TLS at all against anybody who can reach the network. It is almost
# always a debugging shortcut that shipped, and it is the commonest way an
# Android app loses its transport security.
#
# `onReceivedSslError` is the WebView spelling of the same decision, one step
# further along: the platform has already decided the certificate is wrong and
# `handler.proceed()` overrules it. `handler.cancel()` is the correct answer
# and is what the fixture holds beside it.
#
# `checkClientTrusted` is deliberately not reported. It is the server's half,
# nearly every Android implementation of it is empty because the app is not a
# server, and reporting it would put a finding beside every real one and bury
# it.
#
# The empty body is asked as a regex over the capture rather than written as
# `{ }` in the pattern, because in a pattern that is a text comparison against
# one exact spelling. Every spelling of empty matches this, and a comment
# inside the braces does not - a body with a comment in it is somebody saying
# why, which is a decision rather than an oversight.

["fun $F($$$_) { $BODY }"] as $methods
| "^\\{\\s*\\}$" as $doesNothing
| "\\.\\s*proceed\\s*\\(" as $overrules
| scan_ast("*.kt"; $methods) as $all
| ( ($all | of($methods)
     | where_capture("F"; "^checkServerTrusted$") | where_capture("BODY"; $doesNothing))
    + ($all | of($methods)
       | where_capture("F"; "^onReceivedSslError$") | where_capture("BODY"; $overrules)) )
| finding("kotlin-trusts-any-certificate";
    "$F accepts a certificate the platform would have rejected, so anyone on "
    + "the network can read and rewrite this connection")
| report
